1
00:00:08,933 --> 00:00:10,066
[phone ringing]

2
00:00:10,410 --> 00:00:12,045
-[Aziz] Hello?
-[Jerry] Aziz?

3
00:00:12,579 --> 00:00:13,847
[Aziz] Hi, how are you?

4
00:00:14,080 --> 00:00:16,316
-[Jerry] Are you ready?
-[Aziz] Yeah, let's do it.

5
00:00:16,583 --> 00:00:18,685
[Jerry] I've come up here to Boston 
just to do this.

6
00:00:18,766 --> 00:00:19,599
[Aziz] Cool.

7
00:00:19,652 --> 00:00:22,322
I'm parked over on Highway 20
and Southwest.

8
00:00:23,056 --> 00:00:24,190
[Jerry] You're parked?

9
00:00:32,298 --> 00:00:33,867
-Hi!
-[Jerry laughs]

10
00:00:33,933 --> 00:00:36,803
This bus is called "Good Times."
Welcome to Good Times.

11
00:00:36,936 --> 00:00:38,371
Thanks so much for coming down.

12
00:00:38,438 --> 00:00:39,406
[Jerry] I came up.

13
00:00:40,066 --> 00:00:42,900
-[Jerry] How was the show last night?
-[Aziz] It was good. We did two.

14
00:00:43,076 --> 00:00:45,111
[Jerry] So, how are we gonna
get to breakfast?

15
00:00:45,178 --> 00:00:46,279
I mean, we have a bus.

16
00:00:46,366 --> 00:00:47,966
-Can I drive it?
-Sure.

17
00:00:48,047 --> 00:00:49,382
-Let's do it.
-Let's do it.

18
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:52,866
[engine starts]

19
00:01:01,199 --> 00:01:05,466
[Jerry] This is a 2012 Prevost X3-45
in silver.

20
00:01:08,266 --> 00:01:12,700
It's 45 feet long
and has a 500-horsepower Volvo engine.

21
00:01:16,066 --> 00:01:20,399
The brochure for the Prevost X3-45
says it has "classic styling."

22
00:01:21,414 --> 00:01:23,049
I don't know what that means.

23
00:01:25,533 --> 00:01:29,466
It's got... I don't know what it's got.
It's got a lot of stuff.

24
00:01:29,933 --> 00:01:31,266
It's just big, man.

25
00:01:31,333 --> 00:01:35,100
This thing is so big and long.

26
00:01:35,199 --> 00:01:36,733
It's ridiculous.

27
00:01:40,399 --> 00:01:43,100
You feel like you're in
a polished silver building

28
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:45,033
that just got pushed off a cliff.

29
00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:52,933
Hi, I'm Jerry Seinfeld, and this is
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

30
00:01:53,766 --> 00:01:56,566
My guest today is the very
charming Aziz Ansari.

31
00:01:57,300 --> 00:01:58,866
Aziz is a wonderful comedian

32
00:01:58,933 --> 00:02:02,399
and also stars on the NBC show
Parks and Recreation.

33
00:02:03,966 --> 00:02:06,366
Aziz likes to do his road gigs
with a tour bus.

34
00:02:06,426 --> 00:02:07,527
[horn honks]

35
00:02:07,594 --> 00:02:09,362
I'm just gonna go along with that.

36
00:02:16,299 --> 00:02:18,466
Okay. Let's see what I'm gonna do next.

37
00:02:19,299 --> 00:02:20,666
Put the seat belt on.

38
00:02:21,007 --> 00:02:23,176
-Release my air brake.
-[hisses]

39
00:02:24,066 --> 00:02:25,200
Put it in drive.

40
00:02:25,612 --> 00:02:29,115
[beeping]

41
00:02:29,182 --> 00:02:30,116
[engine starts]

42
00:02:34,533 --> 00:02:37,833
Oh, my God.

43
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:39,833
Come on, Aziz.

44
00:02:40,166 --> 00:02:42,266
-You're amazed.
-It's pretty good.

45
00:02:42,862 --> 00:02:45,665
[Aziz] You ever use a tour bus
when you tour, or you always just fly?

46
00:02:45,732 --> 00:02:47,400
I fly or I drive a bus.

47
00:02:48,501 --> 00:02:50,737
[Jerry] This guy needs
to get out of my way.

48
00:02:51,804 --> 00:02:53,840
-[horn honks]
-[Aziz chuckling]

49
00:02:53,966 --> 00:02:55,299
That guy's got a great story.

50
00:02:55,341 --> 00:02:58,945
"Uh, so, I guess Jerry Seinfeld
drives tour buses now."

51
00:02:59,012 --> 00:03:00,113
[both chuckling]

52
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:02,433
[Aziz] There's no one like,
"That's Jerry Seinfeld."

53
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:04,900
They're like, "There's a guy that
really looks like Seinfeld

54
00:03:04,966 --> 00:03:07,299
that drives tour buses in Massachusetts."

55
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,566
[Jerry] The hardest part of driving a bus,
they kept telling me,

56
00:03:10,633 --> 00:03:12,100
is watch out for trees.

57
00:03:12,258 --> 00:03:13,660
[branches scraping]

58
00:03:13,726 --> 00:03:15,762
-See what I mean?
-Where was the tree? I didn't...

59
00:03:15,828 --> 00:03:18,264
-They just come from the sky.
-[Aziz laughing]

60
00:03:18,331 --> 00:03:21,634
[Jerry] I bet if you're a very young bird
your parents would tell that a lot.

61
00:03:21,700 --> 00:03:23,166
"Watch out for trees, son."

62
00:03:30,176 --> 00:03:31,811
[Aziz] Jerry, don't hurt Good Times.

63
00:03:32,212 --> 00:03:33,646
[Jerry] I'm sorry, Ansari.

64
00:03:34,533 --> 00:03:35,633
That's fun to say.

65
00:03:41,221 --> 00:03:43,990
[Aziz] I spent most of my life living
in Bennettsville, South Carolina.

66
00:03:44,066 --> 00:03:45,500
There's nothing going on there.

67
00:03:45,566 --> 00:03:48,399
And how did your parents
just wind up there?

68
00:03:48,466 --> 00:03:51,366
My dad is a gastroenterologist

69
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:54,633
and there was no other gastroenterologist
in that area.

70
00:03:54,700 --> 00:03:57,133
That's a good way to decide where to go.

71
00:03:57,200 --> 00:04:00,733
"Any other gastroenterologists there?
No. I'll be right over."

72
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:07,966
-I'm into...
-Let me concentrate on this turn.

73
00:04:08,033 --> 00:04:09,133
Hang on a second.

74
00:04:13,100 --> 00:04:16,033
What does happen with the bus
is people let you go.

75
00:04:16,089 --> 00:04:17,056
[Aziz chuckles]

76
00:04:17,466 --> 00:04:19,866
You're just a giant flying brick.

77
00:04:23,599 --> 00:04:25,266
This has gotta be weird for you.

78
00:04:30,433 --> 00:04:31,533
It's a little strange.

79
00:04:31,671 --> 00:04:34,707
[Jerry] The most important thing
when you're driving a bus is smoothness.

80
00:04:34,833 --> 00:04:38,366
You gotta be so super smooth
with your steering inputs,

81
00:04:38,633 --> 00:04:40,933
your braking, your accelerating.

82
00:04:41,099 --> 00:04:43,366
[Aziz] If that tree was lower,
what would we do right now?

83
00:04:43,900 --> 00:04:46,333
I would swerve wildly and scream, "Tree!"

84
00:04:51,366 --> 00:04:53,233
Now, I'm gonna back this bus

85
00:04:54,033 --> 00:04:55,900
-into there.
-[Aziz] Whoa.

86
00:04:55,966 --> 00:04:57,133
That's gonna be tough.

87
00:05:05,705 --> 00:05:08,074
[Jerry] I mean, come on. Come on, Ansari.

88
00:05:08,141 --> 00:05:10,877
I thought you were joking. I didn't think
you were gonna back it in.

89
00:05:11,166 --> 00:05:13,566
[Jerry] This is one
of the greatest moments

90
00:05:14,066 --> 00:05:17,733
-in the history of comedy, right here.
-[Aziz chuckling]

91
00:05:22,666 --> 00:05:23,500
Pretty good.

92
00:05:26,325 --> 00:05:27,460
Thanks for the ride.

93
00:05:27,527 --> 00:05:28,961
[man shouts indistinctly]

94
00:05:29,966 --> 00:05:30,800
Thank you.

95
00:05:42,300 --> 00:05:43,300
Please, come in.

96
00:05:43,533 --> 00:05:44,800
Welcome to Brody's.

97
00:05:49,633 --> 00:05:50,800
-[Jerry] Coffee?
-[Aziz] Sure.

98
00:05:57,433 --> 00:05:58,566
-Cheers.
-Cheers.

99
00:05:59,533 --> 00:06:00,466
To comedy.

100
00:06:00,733 --> 00:06:01,833
The ultimate racket.

101
00:06:02,133 --> 00:06:03,800
I don't know if it's a racket. It's hard.

102
00:06:04,733 --> 00:06:06,333
Racket's a pretty easy thing.

103
00:06:06,500 --> 00:06:09,233
"I had this racket.
You work super hard all the time.

104
00:06:09,300 --> 00:06:11,599
And you had to be incredibly lucky
and incredibly talented.

105
00:06:11,637 --> 00:06:12,905
And then you get some money.

106
00:06:13,700 --> 00:06:14,800
It's a great racket."

107
00:06:18,666 --> 00:06:23,200
I wanna stop lying in restaurants
about how the food is to the waiter.

108
00:06:23,333 --> 00:06:25,933
You know how we always say,
"How is everything?"

109
00:06:26,266 --> 00:06:29,066
"Great." And then the place closes,
and they go,

110
00:06:29,133 --> 00:06:32,166
"I wonder why it closed. Was the food bad?
Nobody said anything."

111
00:06:32,333 --> 00:06:33,599
Isn't that what Yelp is?

112
00:06:33,799 --> 00:06:35,099
Yelp is the anonymous...

113
00:06:35,528 --> 00:06:37,430
You don't have to say it
to someone's face.

114
00:06:37,497 --> 00:06:38,998
It's like the Ku Klux Klan.

115
00:06:39,065 --> 00:06:41,367
[chuckling] That's a little bit of a leap.

116
00:06:41,833 --> 00:06:44,666
That'd be a cool study to see racism
across different cultures.

117
00:06:44,766 --> 00:06:46,133
Oh, my... fantastic.

118
00:06:46,172 --> 00:06:48,074
It would be a weird way
to bring us together.

119
00:06:48,141 --> 00:06:49,942
Know how Indian people
do white people's voices?

120
00:06:50,033 --> 00:06:51,533
-No.
-My mom will say,

121
00:06:51,599 --> 00:06:55,000
"Yeah, we just went to the store, we're
coming home." They're like, "Wash, wash.

122
00:06:55,047 --> 00:06:56,983
We just went to the store, wash, wash."

123
00:06:57,066 --> 00:06:58,633
And so they'll, "Wash, wash."

124
00:06:59,099 --> 00:07:01,700
I don't know why
they need to repeat "wash" so many times.

125
00:07:02,833 --> 00:07:04,599
-"Wash, wash, wash."
-See?

126
00:07:04,666 --> 00:07:07,633
-Doesn't it...
-I've never seen a white person do that.

127
00:07:07,799 --> 00:07:12,933
I guess when an Indian person does English
and does an Indian accent,

128
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:16,533
it's very clean and crisp, and then
there's not as many "ow" sounds.

129
00:07:16,633 --> 00:07:18,799
And so I guess that's where
the "wash, wash, wash..."

130
00:07:18,838 --> 00:07:21,441
-Oh, I see.
-I guess that's where that comes from.

131
00:07:21,533 --> 00:07:23,433
The round sound's not so crisp.

132
00:07:23,476 --> 00:07:25,011
Indian accent, very crisp.

133
00:07:25,133 --> 00:07:27,466
I love when people do white people.

134
00:07:27,547 --> 00:07:28,748
I never get tired of it.

135
00:07:28,866 --> 00:07:30,533
The one Eddie Murphy does in Delirious

136
00:07:30,583 --> 00:07:32,452
is a different energy
than people usually do.

137
00:07:32,518 --> 00:07:35,755
The usual energy's like, "Oh, um..."
His is like, "Come on, man. [bleeps]!"

138
00:07:35,833 --> 00:07:40,833
Can you explain to me how is it that
Eddie Murphy, in Beverly Hills Cop...

139
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:44,500
One of the most famous moments
is he's walking in Beverly Hills,

140
00:07:44,666 --> 00:07:48,966
and he sees the two guys
in the crazy leather outfits go by him

141
00:07:49,266 --> 00:07:52,133
and he just stops
and laughs on the street to himself.

142
00:07:52,799 --> 00:07:55,866
And then in his comedy special,
he's wearing that outfit.

143
00:07:55,966 --> 00:07:58,099
-Yeah. Yeah. Isn't that nuts?
-Yeah.

144
00:07:58,144 --> 00:07:59,345
[chuckling]

145
00:08:04,766 --> 00:08:07,033
-So, the school thing, you were not...
-No.

146
00:08:07,099 --> 00:08:09,333
The summer of my freshman year,
I started doing stand-up.

147
00:08:09,400 --> 00:08:10,566
I didn't know what I wanted to do

148
00:08:10,633 --> 00:08:12,233
and I was like,
"I'll go to business school..."

149
00:08:12,299 --> 00:08:14,033
You had to submit a portfolio
or something.

150
00:08:14,099 --> 00:08:15,333
I was like, "I don't wanna to."

151
00:08:15,599 --> 00:08:18,266
Comedians are so lazy about weird things.

152
00:08:18,431 --> 00:08:20,867
Like, being a comedian is very hard.

153
00:08:21,133 --> 00:08:23,133
Louis C.K. told me a similar story.

154
00:08:23,200 --> 00:08:25,133
He wanted to go to NYU film school

155
00:08:25,171 --> 00:08:27,340
but he just couldn't get through
the paperwork.

156
00:08:27,406 --> 00:08:28,374
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

157
00:08:28,733 --> 00:08:31,333
I'm the same way,
I'm so lazy about certain things.

158
00:08:31,400 --> 00:08:34,466
-Right...
-Like, I'll tour for weeks on end,

159
00:08:34,514 --> 00:08:36,449
two shows a night.
Then it's like, uh...

160
00:08:36,633 --> 00:08:38,299
-Can't fill out a form.
-Yeah.

161
00:08:38,551 --> 00:08:40,419
Are you lazy?
Or you feel like you're...

162
00:08:40,533 --> 00:08:44,500
Well, I am lazy with...
Like, this morning, I'm thinking,

163
00:08:44,699 --> 00:08:48,833
"I have the underwear,
maybe I should bring the T-shirt with me

164
00:08:48,900 --> 00:08:49,966
to the shoes.

165
00:08:50,400 --> 00:08:54,266
"Which will save me a trip
back to the drawer to get the T-shirt."

166
00:08:54,333 --> 00:08:55,600
I'll think about that.

167
00:08:55,735 --> 00:08:58,137
"Am I gonna wear a sweater?
If I am, I better get it now.

168
00:08:58,266 --> 00:09:01,033
Otherwise, you gotta walk back here."
It's four steps.

169
00:09:01,100 --> 00:09:02,433
You know, when you're walking,

170
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:04,400
and you realize you're going
the wrong way,

171
00:09:04,466 --> 00:09:07,300
and you turn around to whoever's there.
"Oh, sorry. Wrong way."

172
00:09:07,366 --> 00:09:09,400
'Cause you don't wanna seem crazy,
like a person that's like...

173
00:09:09,466 --> 00:09:10,300
Yeah.

174
00:09:10,933 --> 00:09:12,833
So, you're like, "Oh, God.
I went the wrong way."

175
00:09:13,933 --> 00:09:15,933
Put the finger up. "Okay, I'm..."

176
00:09:16,100 --> 00:09:18,733
-"Not crazy. I left something."
-"I'm not crazy. I made a mistake."

177
00:09:22,966 --> 00:09:24,066
[server] How is everything?

178
00:09:24,133 --> 00:09:24,966
-Good.
-Good.

179
00:09:25,031 --> 00:09:26,332
[server] All right. Good.

180
00:09:27,533 --> 00:09:28,633
Everything really good?

181
00:09:29,933 --> 00:09:32,300
Oh, I forgot to think what's the truth.

182
00:09:32,438 --> 00:09:35,141
So, if I was really honest, I would say,
the blueberries are weird,

183
00:09:35,207 --> 00:09:38,010
there's too much of some kind of liquid
in the oatmeal,

184
00:09:38,100 --> 00:09:39,933
but really good-sized coffee cup.

185
00:09:40,046 --> 00:09:41,847
Would it be worth it
to bring up that stuff?

186
00:09:43,115 --> 00:09:44,050
No.

187
00:09:45,100 --> 00:09:47,466
I was talking to a friend of mine,
who's a little bit older, who has kids.

188
00:09:47,533 --> 00:09:50,300
He's worried about his kids
doing drugs and having sex.

189
00:09:50,366 --> 00:09:53,400
So, he installed spyware
on the kid's phone

190
00:09:54,033 --> 00:09:57,033
that lets him read all the texts
that the kid sends.

191
00:09:57,366 --> 00:09:59,400
That's tough. I'm not gonna have kids.

192
00:09:59,733 --> 00:10:02,000
I don't wanna have to be reading
some 10-year-old's texts.

193
00:10:02,335 --> 00:10:04,203
Well, to me, it's like being a stalker.

194
00:10:04,270 --> 00:10:06,672
It's like, "I can't even
get my own errands done."

195
00:10:06,739 --> 00:10:09,942
The idea of stalking somebody
and following them on their errands...

196
00:10:10,009 --> 00:10:12,845
I go to the dry cleaner.
I gotta watch them go.

197
00:10:12,912 --> 00:10:15,448
So, that's twice the number of errands
you gotta do in one day,

198
00:10:15,514 --> 00:10:17,249
if you're gonna stalk somebody properly.

199
00:10:21,833 --> 00:10:25,400
[Aziz] I read this book about
communication and technology

200
00:10:25,466 --> 00:10:26,466
and how things have changed.

201
00:10:26,533 --> 00:10:27,600
Young people, especially,

202
00:10:27,666 --> 00:10:31,133
are using communication mediums
like email and text and chatting,

203
00:10:31,199 --> 00:10:32,633
where you have a minute
to gather your thoughts

204
00:10:32,699 --> 00:10:34,833
and figure out what you're gonna say,
and write, and rewrite and edit.

205
00:10:34,900 --> 00:10:37,233
So, that muscle that helps you
have spontaneous conversation

206
00:10:37,300 --> 00:10:38,866
isn't as well-built for them.

207
00:10:39,038 --> 00:10:41,507
If you're texting someone romantically,
asking them out,

208
00:10:41,600 --> 00:10:43,766
one thing people can do is
they don't say anything back.

209
00:10:43,866 --> 00:10:46,500
They just give you silence.
We didn't used to do this in the past.

210
00:10:46,566 --> 00:10:49,766
There was never a scenario where, like,

211
00:10:49,833 --> 00:10:51,800
"It was great meeting you.
We should get together.

212
00:10:51,866 --> 00:10:53,000
Maybe grab a drink?"

213
00:10:57,066 --> 00:11:00,400
That just didn't exist. It's so rude.
You would have to say something.

214
00:11:00,626 --> 00:11:03,396
In the past, you get over it eventually.
Out of sight, out of mind.

215
00:11:03,696 --> 00:11:06,632
But now, there's social media.
Facebook, Twitter, Instagram.

216
00:11:06,699 --> 00:11:09,633
So, people become like detectives
and they start opening up tabs.

217
00:11:09,699 --> 00:11:11,466
Like, "Where is this person?
Let's see her.

218
00:11:11,533 --> 00:11:13,766
We got a photo of breakfast.
She had breakfast. Okay.

219
00:11:13,806 --> 00:11:16,575
All right, I got a photo of a zebra here.
Is she at a zoo or something?

220
00:11:16,642 --> 00:11:18,944
-Can we look at the GPS coordinates?"
-[laughs]

221
00:11:19,333 --> 00:11:21,333
And you're just seeing
everything they're doing.

222
00:11:21,400 --> 00:11:22,966
-Right, everyone's the FBI.
-Yeah.

223
00:11:23,015 --> 00:11:24,216
And you haven't heard anything.

224
00:11:24,300 --> 00:11:25,766
You don't know what's happening.

225
00:11:25,833 --> 00:11:27,366
"Are they busy?"
And then you see them

226
00:11:27,553 --> 00:11:29,588
posting a photo of coffee,
and you're like,

227
00:11:29,666 --> 00:11:30,699
"What the [bleeps]?"

228
00:11:31,090 --> 00:11:34,093
And I've put myself out there.
Just say something back to me.

229
00:11:34,160 --> 00:11:35,695
-And you see their phone.
-Yeah.

230
00:11:35,766 --> 00:11:38,566
"There's my message.
I can see my own message on there."

231
00:11:44,266 --> 00:11:46,433
What was the first bit you did on stage?

232
00:11:46,505 --> 00:11:48,908
I remember one of my first bits
that I really liked.

233
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,800
There was a big gay marriage debate
going on

234
00:11:51,844 --> 00:11:55,448
and this one guy said,
"If your neighbor marries a box turtle,

235
00:11:55,514 --> 00:11:57,383
that doesn't affect your everyday life.

236
00:11:57,817 --> 00:11:59,285
But that doesn't mean it's right."

237
00:11:59,366 --> 00:12:02,066
And so, I heard that and I started
doing jokes about the fact that,

238
00:12:02,133 --> 00:12:03,800
why would he pick a box turtle?

239
00:12:03,866 --> 00:12:06,433
Of all things.
That seems like such a specific reference.

240
00:12:06,492 --> 00:12:09,095
This guy's definitely thought about
[bleeps] a box turtle.

241
00:12:11,230 --> 00:12:13,632
There's no way
that's the first thing you jump to.

242
00:12:13,699 --> 00:12:16,068
Why wouldn't you use dog or cat?
Why go to box turtle?

243
00:12:16,166 --> 00:12:19,166
Yeah. That's a long list
until you get to box turtle.

244
00:12:19,205 --> 00:12:24,243
When I was young, in that frustration of,
"[groans] I need to be in a better place,"

245
00:12:24,343 --> 00:12:25,678
I was doing a comic strip

246
00:12:25,745 --> 00:12:29,882
and Lucien Hold, now passed away,
but was still there...

247
00:12:29,949 --> 00:12:32,585
So, this didn't happen after
he passed away? He was still alive?

248
00:12:32,651 --> 00:12:34,253
-He was still alive.
-I see. Okay.

249
00:12:34,320 --> 00:12:36,222
-Glad you clarified that.
-He was still alive.

250
00:12:36,288 --> 00:12:37,156
Okay.

251
00:12:37,423 --> 00:12:39,291
He was like,
"Keep working on your stuff.

252
00:12:39,400 --> 00:12:42,900
Everything will fall into place."
And it's the most sensible advice.

253
00:12:43,033 --> 00:12:44,966
When I was starting out,
doing open mics,

254
00:12:45,030 --> 00:12:48,334
there'd be dudes who would show up with,
"Just got some headshots."

255
00:12:48,566 --> 00:12:51,900
It's like, "Really? You got headshots?
Why?

256
00:12:52,300 --> 00:12:53,466
You're horrible.

257
00:12:53,866 --> 00:12:55,133
There's no reason.

258
00:12:55,199 --> 00:12:57,433
A picture of your face
is not the problem."

259
00:13:02,366 --> 00:13:04,400
[Jerry]
Have your parents come to see you much?

260
00:13:04,599 --> 00:13:07,466
I always invite them when I do
a big show in New York.

261
00:13:07,586 --> 00:13:09,755
If I'm doing Carnegie Hall or something,
I invite them.

262
00:13:09,833 --> 00:13:12,400
I talk about how they had
an arranged marriage.

263
00:13:12,491 --> 00:13:16,195
My dad said he just met my mom,
then a week later, they got married.

264
00:13:16,262 --> 00:13:18,764
-Wow.
-Yeah. Pretty nuts.

265
00:13:19,199 --> 00:13:21,300
I don't know. Maybe not so nuts.

266
00:13:21,500 --> 00:13:23,599
I kind of agree,
because the more I thought about it,

267
00:13:23,666 --> 00:13:26,300
I was like, "This is viewing marriage
as a different thing."

268
00:13:26,466 --> 00:13:28,133
Early 1900s and stuff, like,

269
00:13:28,433 --> 00:13:31,000
people would marry someone that lived
pretty close to them

270
00:13:31,066 --> 00:13:32,166
and they would know them a bit.

271
00:13:32,233 --> 00:13:33,066
Right, right.

272
00:13:33,133 --> 00:13:35,733
It has become this thing of like,
"I've gotta find my soul mate.

273
00:13:35,800 --> 00:13:38,766
I've gotta find the perfect person
that makes me feel this amazing feeling.

274
00:13:39,185 --> 00:13:41,687
Brings out a light inside of me
that's on all the time."

275
00:13:41,766 --> 00:13:44,900
You know, and that actually seems
pretty crazy. That seems insane.

276
00:13:44,966 --> 00:13:47,666
That you think that person is there,
and that you gotta find that person.

277
00:13:47,726 --> 00:13:50,162
-That's funny.
-That seems like a crazier journey.

278
00:13:50,229 --> 00:13:51,864
How old were your parents
when they moved here?

279
00:13:51,933 --> 00:13:53,400
They would have been early 30s.

280
00:13:53,466 --> 00:13:56,666
-And their language was no problem?
-No, no. They spoke English. Yeah.

281
00:13:56,733 --> 00:13:59,266
-Right.
-Or they would just see white people

282
00:13:59,305 --> 00:14:00,940
and go, "Wash, wash, wash."

283
00:14:04,410 --> 00:14:07,313
[Jerry] What's the dream,
like, in 10 years or 15 years,

284
00:14:07,379 --> 00:14:09,415
that you would like
to have accomplished.

285
00:14:09,633 --> 00:14:13,300
My ultimate dream would be, like,
if I was like 90 years old

286
00:14:13,933 --> 00:14:16,833
and I could just drop in
at the Comedy Cellar and just kill it.

287
00:14:16,900 --> 00:14:19,133
That'd be cooler to me
than any other thing.

288
00:14:19,191 --> 00:14:21,227
-That's great.
-The idea to-- Being like...

289
00:14:21,293 --> 00:14:24,163
a really old guy,
still people, like, giving a [bleeps]

290
00:14:24,233 --> 00:14:26,166
about what I had to say on stage.

291
00:14:26,332 --> 00:14:28,434
-I think that'd be so awesome.
-That's really cool.

292
00:14:28,500 --> 00:14:30,099
That makes me feel good to hear that.

293
00:14:32,905 --> 00:14:35,174
-Oh, my gosh. Wow.
-I can't sing to you.

294
00:14:35,774 --> 00:14:38,744
-[man] We're a big fan. Happy birthday.
-Look at that. Aw!

295
00:14:38,833 --> 00:14:42,833
-[man] You each get a hat.
-Thank you very much. That's fantastic.

296
00:14:49,955 --> 00:14:51,290
All right. Let's get out of here.

297
00:14:55,599 --> 00:14:56,433
So now what?

298
00:14:56,528 --> 00:14:58,864
I'm gonna go back to the hotel,
rest for a little bit.

299
00:14:59,833 --> 00:15:02,366
Think about the show. Do it up. 7:00.

300
00:15:02,801 --> 00:15:04,670
Seven o'clock, sometimes,
is kind of annoying,

301
00:15:04,737 --> 00:15:06,605
'cause it's still daylight outside.

302
00:15:06,699 --> 00:15:08,099
-Yeah.
-It's weird.

303
00:15:08,166 --> 00:15:10,433
Comedy has a vampire aspect to it.

304
00:15:10,500 --> 00:15:12,266
You don't wanna do it in the light.

305
00:15:12,333 --> 00:15:15,533
[both laughing]

306
00:15:15,581 --> 00:15:17,683
-[man] Hey, Jerry!
-[Jerry] Yeah. Hey, how you doing?

307
00:15:17,750 --> 00:15:20,352
[man speaking indistinctly]

308
00:15:20,666 --> 00:15:22,266
[Jerry]
I'm gonna check out this guy's truck.

309
00:15:22,321 --> 00:15:23,956
-Wanna see it?
-[Aziz] Yeah. Let's check it out.

310
00:15:25,391 --> 00:15:27,893
[Jerry] So that's a Kenworth,
and what model is it?

311
00:15:27,960 --> 00:15:28,794
W900.

312
00:15:28,861 --> 00:15:32,031
The W900.
And where is that in the Kenworth line?

313
00:15:32,097 --> 00:15:34,566
-[drivers] Top of the line.
-Top of the line!

314
00:15:35,133 --> 00:15:36,833
And how many years will this run?

315
00:15:36,869 --> 00:15:39,038
-Ten years, 12.
-[Jerry] Ten, 12 years?

316
00:15:39,133 --> 00:15:41,599
-[man] We can put a million miles on it.
-A million?

317
00:15:41,666 --> 00:15:44,966
We could drive it for two days
without stopping for fuel, 24 hours a day.

318
00:15:45,066 --> 00:15:47,066
[Jerry] What a great gig.
Now, that's a racket.

319
00:15:47,233 --> 00:15:49,033
Wow, how long is this?

320
00:15:49,099 --> 00:15:50,633
[driver] 77 feet.

321
00:15:50,699 --> 00:15:54,166
Seventy-seven? That's...
This is only 45.

322
00:15:55,233 --> 00:15:56,233
Can I drive it?

323
00:15:56,288 --> 00:15:57,890
-[driver] Absolutely
-[Jerry] Really?

324
00:15:58,433 --> 00:16:00,599
I've always wanted to pull on this handle.

325
00:16:00,759 --> 00:16:02,428
-Hey. How you doing?
-[driver] Good.

326
00:16:02,500 --> 00:16:05,366
Look at this. Go ahead. Give it a shot.

327
00:16:05,464 --> 00:16:07,866
-[blows horn]
-[Jerry laughing]

328
00:16:07,933 --> 00:16:09,702
[man] You feel confident on how to do it?

329
00:16:09,768 --> 00:16:11,470
I do things without confidence.

330
00:16:13,199 --> 00:16:16,066
Those are my air brakes.
Man, that's a clutch.

331
00:16:16,108 --> 00:16:18,110
[alert beeping]

332
00:16:19,500 --> 00:16:20,666
[Jerry] Oh, my God.

333
00:16:21,566 --> 00:16:23,066
Oh, my God.

334
00:16:23,148 --> 00:16:26,051
I'm driving an 18-wheeler!

335
00:16:26,500 --> 00:16:27,933
[screaming] See you later!

336
00:16:28,233 --> 00:16:29,066
[blows horn]

337
00:16:29,900 --> 00:16:30,900
Come on!

338
00:16:36,428 --> 00:16:37,930
-How you doing?
-Good. How are you?

339
00:16:37,997 --> 00:16:39,064
-I'm Jerry.
-Tony.

340
00:16:39,131 --> 00:16:41,166
-So you wanna learn how to drive a bus?
-Yeah.

341
00:16:41,233 --> 00:16:44,670
What's the worst thing you ever heard
happening to a guy driving a bus?

342
00:16:44,766 --> 00:16:47,500
Falling asleep. You got driver's blinds.

343
00:16:47,866 --> 00:16:49,599
Now, you don't drive with
the blinds down.

344
00:16:49,666 --> 00:16:50,500
No.

345
00:16:50,542 --> 00:16:53,812
If you're gonna sleep at the wheel,
you might as well put the blinds down.

346
00:16:53,879 --> 00:16:54,947
[blows horn]

347
00:16:55,033 --> 00:16:57,466
-Oh, yeah. I like that one.
-There you go, you like that one.

348
00:16:57,666 --> 00:16:58,666
That's bus-y.

349
00:16:58,833 --> 00:17:01,233
-What's a "dump lock"?
-No. You don't wanna hit that.

350
00:17:01,286 --> 00:17:03,355
So they actually use the word "dump"?

351
00:17:03,599 --> 00:17:06,266
-Yeah.
-Boy, that's inelegant.

352
00:17:06,325 --> 00:17:07,960
[Tony chuckles]

353
00:17:08,027 --> 00:17:10,763
-Couldn't say "waste" or, uh...
-Uh...

354
00:17:10,829 --> 00:17:13,465
I guess there's no point mincing words
when you're on a bus.

